# <%= current_page.data.title %>

> This is a draft supported pattern. The file transfer service can already
> receive, scan and route clean files. Delivery to a customer-owned S3 bucket
> is not yet available to customers.

Push to S3 is a planned way for the Integration Hub to deliver your processed
files to an S3 bucket that your service owns in an AWS account you administer.
Use this pattern when your service wants to receive files in its own S3 bucket.

## How the pattern works

![A FileActionExecutionRequested event matching this pattern passes through SNS and SQS to a Lambda function. The function assumes an Integration Hub delivery role and copies the object to a customer-owned S3 bucket in another AWS account.](/images/supported-patterns-push-to-s3/push-to-s3.png)

The existing file-transfer journey stays the same until a file is confirmed as
clean. For more detail, see [how files move through the file transfer
service](../how-files-move-through-the-file-transfer-service.html).

For this pattern, the clean-file action will then:

1. route a matching event through the Integration Hub SNS \> SQS \>
   Lambda that serves this pattern
2. use a managed function to assume a dedicated delivery role in the
   Integration Hub file transfer AWS account
3. copy the file to the customer-owned destination bucket
4. write a `FileActionExecutionCompleted` event after the delivery attempt,
   whether the copy succeeds or fails

The function will process a copy as successful only after it has processed the
complete S3 copy response.

The Integration Hub will manage the notification services, queue, function and
delivery role. Your service will manage the destination bucket and control who
can access its files.

The proposed destination object key will match the source object key. Your
service may choose an optional destination prefix during setup. The Integration
Hub will add this prefix before the source object key.

> For example, `your-username/example-file.txt` will become
> `destination-prefix/your-username/example-file.txt` when you use a
> destination prefix.

## Set up your destination bucket

The Integration Hub will provide the Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of its delivery
role. Your team must add a bucket-policy statement that allows that role to put
objects only in the agreed destination bucket and, if used, destination prefix.
The policy does not need to grant the delivery role read, list or delete access
to your objects.

If your bucket uses a customer-managed AWS Key Management Service (KMS) key,
your team must also update its key policy to allow the delivery role to use the
key to encrypt the delivered objects. An AWS managed `aws/s3` key cannot grant
this cross-account access.

For large files, the delivery function may use multipart copy. The Integration
Hub team will tell you about any additional destination-bucket permissions
needed to complete or clean up multipart copies.

Keep encryption at rest and Block Public Access enabled on your destination
bucket. Your service owns the bucket's lifecycle and retention configuration,
as well as its S3 storage, request, data-transfer and KMS costs.

## Information needed for setup

To configure this pattern, the Integration Hub team will need:

* the destination AWS account ID, S3 bucket name and AWS Region
* an optional destination prefix
* the ARN of the customer-managed KMS key, if your bucket uses one
* the environment you need, such as development or production
* your expected file volume, size and delivery frequency
* a technical contact who can update the bucket and KMS key policies

## Discuss this pattern

This pattern is still in design. To discuss whether it is suitable for your
service, post in `#ask-integration-hub` with the setup information above.

Do not include passwords, private keys, file contents or other sensitive data.

## Getting help

For a question about this draft pattern, post in `#ask-integration-hub`.
Include the destination bucket, environment and a description of the issue.